// The gltfimport package: glTF 2.0 models into plain data, in Cone, with no C
// library (cgltf's job). It reads
// what a model is and hands it over as data (meshes, materials, a node
// tree, skins, animations); it draws nothing, simulates nothing, and does not
// import render, so a tool can read a model without linking SDL3. The
// browser takes what it wants from a 'Gltf'.
//
//   import gltfimport use *;
//
//   match loadGltf(path, true) {               // .gltf or .glb; true: decode the images
//     case imm g Ok {
//       // g.meshes[i].primitives[j].mesh      a mesh.Mesh (positions, normals,
//       //                                     uvs, colours, tangents, indices)
//       // g.materials[k]                      metallic-roughness, normal, occlusion, emissive
//       // g.images[n].raster                  an imagedecode.Raster
//       // meshInstances(g)                    every node with a mesh, with its world matrix
//     }
//     case imm e Err { /* e.reason says why */ }
//   }
//
// Also 'loadGltfBytes' (a .glb or a .gltf's JSON text in memory, no external
// files) and 'loadGltfBytesIn' (with a folder for the .bin and image files).
//
// WHAT IS READ
//
//   Files        .gltf with a .bin and images beside it (relative uris,
//                percent-decoded), .gltf with everything embedded (data: uris
//                of base64), and .glb (the JSON chunk, the BIN chunk, images
//                as buffer views). Unreadable files and bad data are errors
//                that say what and where; an image that will not decode is a
//                warning, not a failure, and its Raster stays empty.
//   Accessors    every component type, normalised or not, vectors and
//                matrices (MAT2 and MAT3 with their column padding),
//                interleaved buffer views (byteStride), sparse accessors
//                (with and without a base), checked against their buffers
//                before a byte is read.
//   Meshes       triangle lists, strips and fans (turned into lists), indexed
//                or not; POSITION, NORMAL, TANGENT, TEXCOORD_0 and _1,
//                COLOR_0 (RGB or RGBA), JOINTS_0 and WEIGHTS_0 (weights
//                normalised), morph targets (position, normal and tangent
//                displacements, read and kept, not applied). Points and
//                lines are named in 'warnings' and skipped. Missing normals are
//                made (flat, as the specification says; smooth where the
//                primitive has per-vertex data a split would strand).
//                Texture coordinates are stored (u, 1 - v): glTF's v runs down
//                the image, mesh's up.
//   Materials    metallic-roughness with its three maps, normal scale,
//                occlusion strength, emissive and its map, alpha mode and
//                cut-off, double-sided; KHR_materials_emissive_strength,
//                KHR_texture_transform (kept per texture slot;
//                'bakeTextureTransform' puts a transform into a mesh's uvs
//                for a renderer with none of its own), KHR_materials_unlit.
//                The render hook is a PbrMaterial from the factors and
//                textures: examples/view.cone does it in fifteen lines.
//   Nodes        translation, rotation, scale or a matrix (decomposed), the
//                tree checked (one parent, no cycle), world matrices
//                ('Gltf.worldMatrices', 'worldsOf' for an animated pose).
//   Skins        joints, inverse bind matrices; 'skeletonOf' maps a skin onto
//                bonepose's Skeleton (joints sorted parents-first, with the
//                joint-to-bone permutation so a vertex's JOINTS_0 still
//                finds its bone), 'poseAt' gives a bonepose Pose at a time,
//                'jointMatrices' the matrices that skin a vertex.
//   Animations   translation, rotation, scale and weights channels with
//                linear, step and cubic-spline samplers, as plain data;
//                'sampleVec3', 'sampleQuat' (shortest-arc slerp),
//                'sampleWeights' and 'evaluate' sample them at a time. Nothing
//                plays or loops them.
//   Also         cameras, KHR_lights_punctual lights, scenes, samplers.
//
// REFUSED, by name and with what it would take: KHR_draco_mesh_compression
// (Draco's decoder is about ten thousand lines of C++ with no small part to
// port; its own lane in Cone, or a vendored copy, a new C++ dependency Jon
// would approve) and EXT_meshopt_compression (about six hundred lines of C; a
// day to port). A file that uses one with uncompressed fallback accessors
// loads from the fallback with a warning. Any other extension a file
// REQUIRES that is not listed above is refused; one it merely uses is
// skipped with a warning where it would change a material.
